"CSS Snapshot 2026 (https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260622/)\nciteturn1366view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260622/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4966\nL0: # CSS Snapshot 2026\nL1: \nL2: cite0†W3C Group Note , 22 June 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     cite1†https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260622/ L7: Latest published version:\nL8:     cite2†https://www.w3.org/TR/css-2026/ L9: Editor's Draft:\nL10:     cite3†https://drafts.csswg.org/css-2026/†drafts.csswg.org L11: Previous Versions:\nL12:     cite4†https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260326/ L13: History:\nL14:     cite5†https://www.w3.org/standards/history/css-2026/ L15: Feedback:\nL16:     cite6†CSSWG Issues Repository†github.com L17: Editors:\nL18:     cite7†Tab Atkins Jr.†xanthir.com (Google)\nL19:     cite8†Elika J. Etemad / fantasai†fantasai.inkedblade.net (Apple)\nL20:     cite9†Florian Rivoal†florian.rivoal.net (Invited Expert)\nL21:     cite10†Chris Lilley†svgees.us (W3C)\nL22:     Sebastian Zartner (sebastianzartner@gmail.com) (Invited Expert)\nL23: Suggest an Edit for this Spec:\nL24:     cite11†GitHub Editor†github.com L25: cite12†Copyright © 2026 cite13†World Wide Web Consortium . W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} cite14†liability , cite15†trademark and cite16†permissive document license rules apply.\nL26: \nL27: * * *\nL28: ## Abstract\nL29: \nL30: This document collects together into one definition all the specs that together form the current state of Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) as of 2026. The primary audience is CSS implementers, not CSS authors, as this definition includes modules by specification stability, not Web browser adoption rate.\nL31: \nL32: cite17†CSS is a language for describing the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on screen, on paper, etc.\nL33: ## Status of this document\nL34: \nL35: This section describes the status of this document at the time of its publication. A list of current W3C publications and the latest revision of this technical report can be found in the cite18†W3C standards and drafts index. L36: \nL37: This document was published by the cite19†CSS Working Group as a Group Note using the cite20†Note track . Group Notes are not endorsed by W3C nor its Members.\nL38: Please send feedback by cite21†filing issues in GitHub†github.com (preferred), including the spec code “css-2026” in the title, like this: “[css-2026] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are cite22†archived†lists.w3.org . Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (cite23†archived†lists.w3.org ) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL39: \nL40: This document is governed by the cite24†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document .\nL41: The cite25†15 September 2020 W3C Patent Policy does not carry any licensing requirements or commitments on this document.\nL42: \nL43: This document represents the state of CSS as of 2026.\nL44: ## Table of Contents\nL45:   1. cite26†1 Introduction L46:     1. cite27†1.1 What is CSS? L47:     2. cite28†1.2 Background: The W3C Process and CSS L48:   2. cite29†2 Classification of CSS Specifications L49:     1. cite30†2.1 Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) — The Official Definition L50:     2. cite31†2.2 Reliable Candidate Recommendations L51:     3. cite32†2.3 Fairly Stable Modules with limited implementation experience L52:     4. cite33†2.4 Modules with Rough Interoperability L53:     5. cite34†2.5 CSS Levels L54:     6. cite35†2.6 CSS Profiles L55:   3. cite36†3 Requirements for Responsible Implementation of CSS L56:     1. cite37†3.1 Partial Implementations L57:     2. cite38†3.2 Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L58:       1. cite39†3.2.1 Experimentation and Unstable Features L59:       2. cite40†3.2.2 Proprietary and Non-standardized Features L60:       3. cite41†3.2.3 Market Pressure and De Facto Standards L61:     3. cite42†3.3 Implementations of CR-level Features L62:   4. cite43†4 Safe to Release pre-CR Exceptions L63:   5. cite44†5 The text/css media type L64:   6. cite45†6 Security Considerations L65:   7. cite46†7 Privacy Considerations L66:   8. cite47†8 Indices L67:     1. cite48†8.1 Terms Index L68:     2. cite49†8.2 Selector Index L69:     3. cite50†8.3 At-Rule Index L70:     4. cite51†8.4 Property Index L71:     5. cite52†8.5 Values Index L72:   9. cite53†9 Acknowledgements L73:   10. cite54†Conformance L74:     1. cite55†Document conventions L75:     2. cite56†Conformance classes L76:     3. cite57†Partial implementations L77:       1. cite58†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L78:     4. cite59†Non-experimental implementations L79:   11. cite60†References L80:     1. cite61†Normative References L81:     2. cite62†Non-Normative References L82: ## 1. Introduction\nL83: \nL84: When the first CSS specification was published, all of CSS was contained in one document that defined CSS Level 1. CSS Level 2 was defined also by a single, multi-chapter document. However for CSS beyond Level 2, the CSS Working Group chose to adopt a modular approach, where each module defines a part of CSS, rather than to define a single monolithic specification. This breaks the specification into more manageable chunks and allows more immediate, incremental improvement to CSS.\nL85: Since different CSS modules are at different levels of stability, the CSS Working Group has chosen to publish this profile to define the current scope and state of Cascading Style Sheets as of 2026.\nL86: ### 1.1. What is CSS?\nL87: Cascading Style Sheets (CSS)\nL88:     CSS is a language for writing cite63†style sheets , and is designed to describe the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on a variety of media. CSS is used to describe the presentation of a cite64†source document , and usually does not change the underlying semantics expressed by its cite65†document language .\nL89: Style sheet\nL90:     A set of rules that specify the presentation of a document. Style sheets are written by an cite66†Author , and interpreted by a cite67†user agent , to present the document to the cite68†User .\nL91: Source document\nL92:     The document to which one or more style sheets apply. A source document’s structure and semantics are encoded using a cite65†document language (e.g., HTML, XHTML, or SVG).\nL93: Author\nL94:     An author is a person who writes documents and associated style sheets. An authoring tool is a cite67†user agent that generates style sheets.\nL95: User\nL96:     A user is a person who interacts with a user agent to view, hear, or otherwise use the document.\nL97: User agent (UA)\nL98:     A user agent is any program that interprets a document and its associated cite63†style sheets on behalf of a cite68†user . A cite67†user agent may display a document, read it aloud, cause it to be printed, convert it to another format, etc. For the purposes of the CSS specifications, a user agent is one that supports and interprets cite69†Cascading Style Sheets as defined in these specifications.\nL99: ### 1.2. Background: The W3C Process and CSS\nL100: \nL101: This section is non-normative.\nL102: \nL103: In the cite70†W3C Process , a Recommendation-track document passes through three levels of stability, summarized below:\nL104: \nL105: Working Draft (WD)\nL106: \nL107: \nL108: This is the design phase of a W3C spec. The WG iterates the spec in response to internal and external feedback.\nL109: The first official Working Draft is designated the “First Public Working Draft” (FPWD). In the CSSWG, publishing FPWD indicates that the Working Group as a whole has agreed to work on the module, roughly as scoped out and proposed in the editor’s draft.\nL110: The transition to the next stage is sometimes called “Last Call Working Draft” (LCWD) phase. The CSSWG transitions Working Drafts once we have resolved all known issues, and can make no further progress without feedback from building tests and implementations.\nL111: This “Last Call for Comments” sets a deadline for reporting any outstanding issues, and requires the WG to specially track and address incoming feedback. The comment-tracking document is the Disposition of Comments (DoC). It is submitted along with an updated draft for the Director’s approval, to demonstrate wide review and acceptance.\nL112: Candidate Recommendation (CR)\nL113:     This is the testing phase of a W3C spec. Notably, this phase is about using tests and implementations to test the specification: it is not about testing the implementations. This process often reveals more problems with the spec, and so a Candidate Recommendation will morph over time in response to implementation and testing feedback, though usually less so than during the design phase (WD).\nL114: Demonstration of two correct, independent implementations of each feature is required to exit CR, so in this phase the WG builds a test suite and generates implementation reports.\nL115: \nL116: The transition to the next stage is “Proposed Recommendation” (PR). During this phase the W3C Advisory Committee must approve the transition to REC.\nL117: Recommendation (REC)\nL118:     This is the completed state of a W3C spec and represents a maintenance phase. At this point the WG only maintains an errata document and occasionally publishes an updated edition that incorporates the errata back into the spec.\nL119: An Editor’s Draft is effectively a live copy of the editors’ own working copy. It may or may not reflect Working Group consensus, and can at times be in a self-inconsistent state. (Because the publishing process at W3C is time-consuming and onerous, the cite71†Editor’s Draft is usually the best (most up-to-date) reference for a spec.\nL120: Efforts are currently underway to reduce the friction of publishing, so that official drafts will be regularly up-to-date and Editor’s Drafts can return to their original function as scratch space.)\nL121: ## 2. Classification of CSS Specifications\nL122: \nL123: A list of all CSS modules, stable and in-progress, and their statuses can be found at the cite72†CSS Current Work page .\nL124: ### 2.1. Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) — The Official Definition\nL125: \nL126: This profile includes only specifications that we consider stable and for which we have enough implementation experience that we are sure of that stability.\nL127: \nL128: Note: This is not intended to be a CSS Desktop Browser Profile: inclusion in this profile is based on feature stability only and not on expected use or Web browser adoption. This profile defines CSS in its most complete form.\nL129: As of 2026, Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) is defined by the following specifications.\nL130: cite73†CSS Level 2, latest revision (including errata) cite74†[CSS2] L131:     This defines the core of CSS, parts of which are overridden by later specifications. We recommend in particular reading cite75†Chapter 2 , which introduces some of the basic concepts of CSS and its design principles.\nL132: cite76†CSS Syntax Module Level 3 cite77†[CSS-SYNTAX-3] L133:     Replaces CSS2§4.1, CSS2§4.2, CSS2§4.4, and CSS2§G, redefining how CSS is parsed.\nL134: cite78†CSS Style Attributes cite79†[CSS-STYLE-ATTR] L135:     Defines how CSS declarations can be embedded in markup attributes.\nL136: cite80†Media Queries Level 3 cite81†[CSS3-MEDIAQUERIES] L137:     Replaces CSS2§7.3 and expands on the syntax for media-specific styles.\nL138: cite82†CSS Conditional Rules Module Level 3 cite83†[CSS-CONDITIONAL-3] L139:     Extends and supersedes CSS2§7.2, updating the definition of cite84†@media rules to allow nesting and introducing the cite85†@supports rule for feature-support queries.\nL140: cite86†Selectors Level 3 cite87†[SELECTORS-3] L141:     Replaces CSS2§5 and CSS2§6.4.3, defining an extended range of selectors.\nL142: cite88†CSS Namespaces Module Level 3 cite89†[CSS3-NAMESPACE] L143:     Introduces an cite90†@namespace†drafts.csswg.org rule to allow namespace-prefixed selectors.\nL144: cite91†CSS Cascading and Inheritance Level 4 cite92†[CSS-CASCADE-4] L145:     Extends and supersedes CSS2§1.4.3 and CSS2§6, as well as cite93†[CSS-CASCADE-3] . Describes how to collate style rules and assign values to all properties on all elements. By way of cascading and inheritance, values are propagated for all properties on all elements.\nL146: cite94†CSS Values and Units Module Level 3 cite95†[CSS-VALUES-3] L147:     Extends and supersedes CSS2§1.4.2.1, CSS2§4.3, and CSS2§A.2.1–3, defining CSS’s property definition syntax and expanding its set of units.\nL148: cite96†CSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 1 cite97†[CSS-VARIABLES-1] L149:     Introduces cascading variables as a new primitive value type that is accepted by all CSS properties, and custom properties for defining them.\nL150: cite98†CSS Box Model Module Level 3 cite99†[CSS-BOX-3] L151:     Replaces CSS2§8.1, §8.2, §8.3 (but not §8.3.1), and §8.4.\nL152: cite100†CSS Color Module Level 4 cite101†[CSS-COLOR-4] L153:     Extends and supersedes CSS2§4.3.6, CSS2§14.1, and CSS2§18.2, also extends and supersedes cite102†[CSS-COLOR-3] , introducing an extended range of color spaces beyond sRGB, extended color values, and CSS Object Model extensions for color. Also defines the cite103†opacity property.\nL154: cite104†CSS Backgrounds and Borders Module Level 3 cite105†[CSS-BACKGROUNDS-3] L155:     Extends and supersedes CSS2§8.5 and CSS2§14.2, providing more control of backgrounds and borders, including layered background images, image borders, and drop shadows.\nL156: cite106†CSS Images Module Level 3 cite107†[CSS-IMAGES-3] --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Flexible Box Layout Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/)\nciteturn1366view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/#resolve-flexible-lengths\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3589\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Fragmentation Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-break-3/)\nciteturn1366view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-break-3/#breaking-rules\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 694\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Fonts Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/)\nciteturn1366view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/#cluster-matching\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4953\n"
